===[ Why XGboost does not the catch the trend ? ]===================================
* XGboost does not understand 'time' or 'trend'
  - It only learns:
    . "When inputs look like this -> output is That"

- It has no concept of:
  . past -> future
  . growth over time
  . upward or downward direction


- What your model actually sees:
  ___________________________________
  | lag_1 | lag_7 | month | weekday |
  |-------|-------|-------|---------|
  | 120   | 110   | Jan   | Mon     |
  |_______|_______|_______|_________|

- "If lag_1 is high and month = jan -> predict high value"

-> What it does NOT See:
    . "this series is growing every year"
    . "values increase as time passes"
    . "there is an upward slope"
  - Because nothing in your features says:
    . "this row comes later in time than that row"


-> Why Trend Fails in forecasting:
  - Step 1 (training)
  - When you forecast long-term
    . It learns local `relationships`:
    . lag -> next value

  - Step 2 (forecasting)
  - If early predictions drift slightly down:
    . You feed predictions back as lags
    . Now the model is basically doing:
      "based on my own previous predictions... what comes next"

  - If early predictions drift slightly down:
    . That becomes the new input
    . And it keeps going down

  - This is why you often get:
    "downward or flat drift instead of upward trend"

-> [ The Core Problem ]
  - XGboost is blind to this idea:
    "time is moving forward"

  - So it cannot learn:
    value = trend + seasonality + noise

  - Unless you explictly tell it:
    . "This is time"
    . "This is progression"
    . "This si growth direction"


===[ Solutions ]===
- Adding time features:
  [ t = 1, 2, 3, 4, ... ]

- Now XGboost can learn:
  "as t increases -> value increases"

- So it finally has 'coordicate system for trend'.
- Without it:
  [ every row looks like an isolated snapshot ]

  # INFO:[ Analogy: ]==========================================================
  - XGboost is like a chef who only sees ingredients in a bowl
  - It does NOT Know the recipe order
  - So it cannot understand "this dish gets spicier over time"unless you
    label time explicity
  - Lag features = local memory
  - XGBoost = pattern rules on rows
  - No time feature  = no concept of 'direction'
  - Result = trend is not learned, only local correlations are


